CAT = "dktrBrakes"; SEG = "BRK"
# Afsnit 1.1 and 1.2: steering, power steering, ESC, air brakes, parking and supplementary brakes.
Q = [
("Your lorry has hydraulic power steering. When does the power assistance work?",
 "Only with the engine running", ["Only above walking pace", "Only when the lights are on", "Also with the engine switched off"],
 "The power steering pump is driven by the engine, so the assistance only works with the engine running. An electric pump also needs the engine started."),
("The power steering fails while you are driving. What is true?",
 "It is too heavy to drive on safely", ["The steering locks solid at once", "You will notice no real difference", "The service brakes stop working"],
 "You can still steer with the steering gear alone, but it is so heavy that driving on is dangerous and unlawful."),
("Which newer lorries are exempt from the rule that they must have ESC?",
 "Lorries with more than 3 axles", ["Lorries with only 2 axles", "Lorries under 12 tonnes", "Lorries fitted with air suspension"],
 "Newer lorries and buses must have Electronic Stability Control, but lorries with more than three axles are exempt."),
("What does Electronic Stability Control (ESC) do?",
 "Brakes wheels to prevent skids", ["Holds a set gap to the car ahead", "Limits the top speed to 90 km/h", "Stops wheelspin when moving off"],
 "ESC brakes one or more wheels to reduce the risk of the vehicle skidding or overturning. Wheelspin when moving off is the job of ASR."),
("The front tyres of your lorry show uneven, lopsided wear. What may this point to?",
 "Wrongly set steering geometry", ["Too little air in the brake tanks", "A faulty ABS wheel sensor", "A weak starter battery"],
 "Uneven or skewed tyre wear can be a sign that the steering is wrongly adjusted. Faulty shock absorbers or an axle shifted on its springs can do the same."),
("There is noticeable free play in the steering wheel. What can cause it?",
 "Play in the steering joints", ["Low pressure in the rear tyres", "A worn clutch plate", "Too much power steering oil"],
 "Free play can come from play in the steering linkage, a steering box loose on the chassis, or play in the front wheel bearings or suspension."),
("The power steering makes a whining noise as you turn. What is a likely cause?",
 "Too little steering oil", ["Worn brake linings", "A loose exhaust bracket", "A failing alternator"],
 "Noise from power steering can be caused by a lack of oil in the system. Check the fluid level in the reservoir."),
("In an air brake system, what is the job of the compressor?",
 "To build up air pressure", ["To pump the steering oil", "To dry the air in the tanks", "To cool the engine's intake air"],
 "The compressor, driven by the engine, draws in air through a filter and compresses it to build pressure in the supply circuit. Drying the air is the air dryer's job."),
("Why does an air brake system have an air dryer?",
 "To stop ice forming in winter", ["To raise the working pressure", "To save fuel on long trips", "To make the compressor quieter"],
 "Moisture in the compressed air can freeze in winter and cause brake failure. The dryer removes it before the air reaches the tanks."),
("You find water in the air tanks. What is this a sign of?",
 "A faulty air dryer", ["A worn compressor", "Brakes needing adjustment", "An overfilled air tank"],
 "Water in the tanks can mean the air dryer is not working. A worn compressor shows as a long pump-up time instead."),
("At what point must the low air pressure warning come on?",
 "Below about 65% of set pressure", ["Below about 90% of set pressure", "Below about 50% of set pressure", "Below about 25% of set pressure"],
 "The warning must come on when supply pressure falls below two thirds (65%) of the brake pressure the manufacturer specifies, and go off above it."),
("Building up air pressure takes far longer than it used to. What can cause this?",
 "A worn compressor or a leak", ["Water in the air tanks", "A faulty load-sensing valve", "Brakes needing adjustment"],
 "A long pump-up time can mean a worn compressor, a blocked air filter or leaks in the system. Water in the tanks gives a shorter time than usual."),
("Pump-up time is shorter than usual, but pressure drops a lot on full braking. What may be wrong?",
 "Water or dents in tanks", ["A leaking hose to the brakes", "A blocked air filter", "Worn brake linings"],
 "Water or dents take up space in the air tanks, so they fill faster but hold less air, giving a large pressure drop when you brake hard."),
("With the engine stopped, you make one full brake application. How much should the pressure normally drop at most?",
 "0.5 bar", ["0.1 bar", "1.5 bar", "3 bar"],
 "As a rule one full application with the engine off should not use more than 0.5 bar. A bigger drop can mean the brakes need adjusting."),
("Your brakes use much more air than normal. What is a likely cause?",
 "The brakes need adjusting", ["The compressor is worn", "The air dryer has failed", "The lorry is lightly loaded"],
 "Higher air use than normal points to too long a stroke in the brake chambers, usually because the brakes are not adjusted. It also delays and weakens braking."),
("What does the load-sensing valve (ALB valve) do?",
 "Matches braking force to the load", ["Stops the wheels locking", "Holds the lorry when parked", "Brakes the lorry whenever you reverse"],
 "The ALB valve regulates brake pressure according to how heavily the vehicle is loaded. Stopping wheel lock is the ABS system's job."),
("The rear wheels lock under moderate braking. What may be the cause?",
 "A faulty load-sensing valve", ["Water in the air tanks", "Low pressure in the rear tyres", "A worn air compressor"],
 "Rear wheels locking under moderate braking can mean the ALB valve is wrongly set or defective, so it sends too much pressure to the rear brakes."),
("How do you do a leak test on air brakes?",
 "Brake pressed, engine off, listen", ["Brake hard from 30 km/h", "Pump until the warning lamp lights", "Release the spring brake and wait"],
 "Bring the system to full working pressure, press the brake pedal fully, stop the engine and listen for leaks. No hissing and no pressure drop means it is tight enough."),
("How does a spring brake (fjederbremse) apply the brakes?",
 "A spring pushes them on", ["Air pressure pushes them on", "A cable from a hand lever", "The engine brake holds them"],
 "Operating the valve lets a spring act mechanically on the wheel brakes. Applying it releases air from the brake cylinder, which you can hear."),
("How does the engine brake (motorbremse) slow the lorry?",
 "It blocks the exhaust, cuts fuel", ["It brakes every wheel with compressed air", "It applies the spring brakes", "It brakes only the front axle"],
 "A flap blocks the exhaust gas while the fuel supply is cut, which gives a braking effect on the driven wheels."),
("What does a reversing brake (bakbremse) do?",
 "Brakes if a rear sensor is touched", ["Holds the lorry still on hills", "Limits how fast you can reverse", "Sounds an alarm whenever you reverse"],
 "With reverse gear engaged, the system brakes the lorry automatically using the service or parking brake when a sensor strip at the rear is touched."),
("An obstacle appears ahead and the AEBS system reacts. What does it do first?",
 "It warns the driver", ["It brakes fully at once", "It steers round the obstacle", "It switches off the engine"],
 "AEBS first warns the driver with light, sound or vibration (the collision warning phase) and can then apply the service brake (the emergency braking phase)."),
("On what gradient must the parking brake be able to hold the lorry?",
 "18%", ["8%", "12%", "25%"],
 "The parking brake must hold the vehicle on a road with an 18% gradient and stay applied."),
("On a system without a gauge, how fast must the compressor fill empty tanks until the warning stops?",
 "Within 3 minutes", ["Within 1 minute", "Within 10 minutes", "Within 30 seconds"],
 "Without a gauge the compressor must fill empty tanks in under 3 minutes until the warning lamp goes out. With a gauge, 65% of the set pressure must be reached in that time."),
]
